course: dictionary
lesson: 10549
title: Unarmed: without a weapon
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unarmed is built from two parts. Un- means not, and armed means carrying a weapon. So unarmed means not carrying a weapon. When a word has a clear beginning like un-, look at the parts first.
explain: «They were unarmed, and taken by surprise, and what can two animals do against hundreds?» The question is the clue. Two animals against hundreds, with no weapons, cannot fight back, so unarmed makes them weak and in danger.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. «Mole and Badger were sitting by the fire, unarmed and taken by surprise» becomes «Mole and Badger were sitting by the fire, not carrying any weapons and taken by surprise». It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Unarmed can also describe a fight. Unarmed combat means fighting without weapons. Ask yourself: is someone carrying a weapon, or is the fight happening without any?
fact: Unarmed means not carrying a weapon.
fact: Un- at the start of a word means not, so unarmed is the opposite of armed.
fact: «Unarmed combat» means fighting without the use of weapons.
fact: In the UK, Ireland and New Zealand, most police officers are unarmed: they do not carry guns.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Mole goes into the Wild Wood alone and unarmed, so he is in danger.
exercise: «Mole and Badger were sitting by the fire, unarmed and taken by surprise.» What does unarmed mean here? | Warm and well fed | Very angry and loud | *Not carrying any weapons | Hiding in the cellar
exercise: Look at the word parts: un- means not, and armed means carrying a weapon. What does unarmed mean? | Very strong | *Not carrying a weapon | Carrying a weapon | Hungry
exercise: «They were unarmed, and taken by surprise, and what can two animals do against hundreds?» Find and copy one word that means not carrying weapons. | surprise | animals | *unarmed | hundreds
test: Why is Mole in danger in the Wild Wood? | He is asleep under a tree | He has a map and a torch | He is carrying too many weapons | *He goes there alone and unarmed
test: Why are most police officers in the UK, Ireland and New Zealand called unarmed? | They only work in schools | They carry only swords | They never go out at night | *They do not carry guns
exam: Why would attacking an unarmed person be especially wrong? | They are too far away to reach | They have too many friends nearby | *They cannot fight back | They are carrying a shield
